Date: Thu, 13 May 93 14:22:58 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: TECH: Numerical Speculation

Hal Finney writes:
>I would not criticize numerical speculation in general, but rather
>speculation as it is often practiced, which gives a false sense of
>accuracy.  Paul gave the example of the Drake equation for the number of
>civilizations in the galaxy.  This did make its assumptions fairly
>explicit, and perhaps could be said to help us remember what we do and
>don't know.  The problem was, we really know almost nothing about many
>terms in that equation.  And the equation didn't make that clear, at
>least in the form in which it is often presented. ...
>
>I don't think Paul's point was that numerical speculations are inherently
>invalid.  It is rather that when we deal with a domain which has so many
>more unknowns than knowns, properly done numerical analyses will produce
>results with such massive error bars as to be virtually useless.  In this
>domain, verbal arguments are more appropriate because they remind us that
>we are only waving our hands.

Yes it helps to carry an uncertainty estimate for each parmeter in a
model.  And people have a known cognitive bias of overconfidence.  But
number estimates with very large error bars are not at all useless;
they can tell us just how little we know.  Words and numbers have
complimentary uses in speculation.  To avoid numbers merely to signal
a high uncertainty is a very expensive signal; many of us come from
technical cultures where this uncertainty is signaled more cheaply in
other ways.

As you might expect, I think it would be great to form consensus on
such estimates through betting markets.  Such markets could create
probability distributions over such parameters (eg # alien civils,
bits/brain).  If you thought the consensus was too certain, you could
buy assets near the tails of the distribution.  Thus a social process
could correct for overconfidence, and still return numbers, even under
very high uncertainty.  

Robin Hanson


