Date: Thu, 13 May 93 19:12:11 EDT
From: chrism@ksr.com
Subject: The Incredibly High Cost of High Tech

> 
> 
> chrism@ksr.com says:
> > 
> > It depends on the style of asynchrounous design that you are
> > practicing.  You only have "synchronizers" if you have a
> > free-running clock that defines the timing of (some portion of) the
> > system.  It's true that you can't get around metastability, but you
> > only have synchronizer failure when have to make a decision in a
> > fixed amount of time (in time for the next clock edge) and your
> > synchronizer doesn't make it.  ...
> 
> ...
> 
> This one comes back to bite EEs in the buttocks over and over again.
> As an example, there is the famous case of the first PDP-11 to use
> DRAMs in which the refresh cycle was triggered by a one-shot that
> obviously fired asynchronously to the rest of the system. The first
> machines would crash frequently for no apparent reason -- until the
> engineers discovered that the synchronizer arbitrating the memory
> access was periodically going metastable for long periods. Ugh.

Actually, I believe that you are thinking of the unibus interface on the
PDP-11/45.  The unibus (memory and I/O interface bus) is asynchronous to the
processor, and the '45 was enough faster than previous machines that the average
time required by ttl flip-flops to exit the metastable state became a
significant source of "soft errors" for the first time.

Again, I agree that metastability is unavoidable, but repeat that it is only a
source of failures when the output of the state element must be valid by the
next transition of a free running clock.  Note also that it is possible to
reduce the probability that a failure actually occurs to an arbitrarily low
value.  When I worked at DEC I did the theoretical analysis and simulation to
determine the failure rate, due to synchronizer failure, of the bus interface
for the "J11", (damn, I forget the product name!) which was DEC's first CMOS
PDP-11 microprocessor.  We were able to get the expected failure rate down to on
the order of once in a thousand years.

> A certain research machine I was familiar with at Columbia was full of
> asynchronous elements.  This was why the machine would hang after mere
> seconds of operation, according to a friend of mine. (The hangs were
> well known, but never hurt DoD funding. Any sufficiently advanced
> technology is indistinguishable from a rigged demo, you know...)
> 
> Proper design can fix all this, of course, but its rarely actually
> done. This leads to pain and suffering all the time.

Certainly it's not usually done by people building research toys^H^H^H^H
machines.  Hell, I've seen quite a few research-oriented hardware projects and
it is usually impossible to get the designers to design using worst-case (as
opposed to "typical") component specs!

> > ... the whole point of asynchronous design is
> > to make the timing problems easier.  If you properly define the
> > signalling between modules, and the composition rules for
> > interconnecting them, then any legal interconnection of modules will
> > "make timing."  Which is to say, that it will exhibit stable
> > behavior at a rate determined by the slowest module.  And you've
> > eliminated the need for distributing a global clock to every point
> > in the system ...
> 
> I fully agree that you can reduce the problem by modularization.
> However, things still can get tricky to track down.

My point is not just that you can reduce the problem by use of modularization.
I claim that with the proper choice of primitive design elements asynchronous
design is correct-by-construction, and hence it may actually be easier than an
equivalent synchronous design.  On the down side, it costs gates/area/pins, and
existing design tools are not well suited for this style of design.  When it was
easy to distribute a global clock, synchronous design was obviously more
efficient.  But scaling keeps pushing the performance up, and making
communication within a system steadily more costly relative to logic, and I
believe that you will see more and more asynchronous design creeping in as time
goes on.  

We are already seeing signs that strictly synchronous design is becoming
prohibitively expensive as system performance increases.  Personal computers and
workstations where the processor internals run at a multiple of the external
clock rate have become common.  On these systems the processor clock is usually
generated internally and then sync'ed up to the outside world with a
phase-locked loop.  Imagine a future large-scale computer with thousands of
processor, each capable of executing hundreds of millions of instructions per
second.  One way to organize the system would be as a synchronous system with a
hierarchy of clock rates.  For instance, the internals of the processors run at
~1Ghz, the circuit boards that make of the system run on a 200Mhz clock, the
chassis are clocked at a lower rate still, and so on for signalling between
chassis.  An alternative would be to eliminate the use of a clock to indicate
when a signal at some level of the hierarchy is valid, and to use handshakes
between (asynchronous) subsystems instead.  Indeed, it is even possible to have
a subsystem which uses a synchronous clock internally and communicates
asynchronously at the interface, and does not suffer from synchronizer failure
at all.  The secret is to start and stop the internal clock -- using a circuit
known as "Chuckie's hourglass" (after Chuck Seitz of Caltech.) -- by starting
and stopping the internal clock rather than attempting to synchronize the
external signals to a fixed rate clock.

> > > The course recalled with the greatest fear and loathing of all as I
> > > contemplated my graduate corsework was asynchronous digital circuits.
> > > Pain and suffering beyond the capacity of humans to describe.
> ...
> A course in such circuits is a prerequisite to passing the hardware
> qual in the Columbia CS PhD program. Its a bear.

Agreed, however it is possible to design a complete (and correct) asynchronous
computer and never use those techniques once.

This has run on longer than I intended, and I'm not sure of its ER (extropian
relevance), so perhaps we should take it to email...

-chrism



