Brian Holt Hawthorne:

> I don't know of any studies on the maximal learning rate, but given
> the fact that a human is capable of being exposed to visual stimulus
> (such as opening your eyes for a split second), and remembering the
> entire  scene in full-color, I'd say the data rate has got to be
> pretty high!

Do we recall images, or do we *think* we recall images?  I remember (!)
reading of an experiment where yellow and blue objects (vehicles, as it
happened) were deliberately blurred in the memory of test subjects.  The
experimenters found that in subjects to whom it was suggested that
they'd only seen one such object (colour unspecified) a significant
number recalled it's colour as green.  They added the colours together
as part of the data compression process.  Our memories undergo
successive stages of data compression with time and are unpacked when
recalled.  Which is why it's possible to recall, in visual detail,
incidents that never happened.
> 
> The one-bit-per-second claim is not only not backed up by research,
> but it is easily proven false by any human.

I have seen documentary evidence for this claim (although I think the
claim has an error/subject range of 1-2 bits/second into long term
storage).  If necessary I can find the references (I remember where to
look!).

