Date: Thu, 13 May 93 18:34:33 MDT
From: Mark_Muhlestein@Novell.COM (Mark Muhlestein)
Subject: AI: Synaptic Storage Requirements

I have found the discussion interesting and "useful."  I agree with Tim
May's comments, and Derek, you should not feel put down just because we
don't currently have hard numbers to plug in.  It is interesting to
speculate about, in any case.

Something no one mentioned that I find interesting in this connection
is the observation made by surgeons doing searches for the focus of
epileptic seizures who directly stimulated the exposed cortices of
alert patients.  There is an interesting discussion of this in _The Man
Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat_ by Oliver Sacks.  (If anyone is
interested in this I can dig up the reference.)

Basically, what they found was that it was possible to reliably evoke a
full replay of certain experiences the person had had by probing
different regions.  Some of the experiences were "special," like
birthday parties, etc., but many were just seemingly random past
moments that the patient couldn't normally recall, but which were
perceived as veridical past experiences by the patient.  The patients
sometimes reported just hearing a voice, or some random pop song, but
often they reported a full "doubling" of experience in which the past
moment was "re-lived" in every sensory modality.

This evidence, combined with reports of some near death experiences in
which the person had the sensation of reviewing their entire lives,
makes me wonder just how much is really being stored in the brain.

I don't really think that everything I experience is permanently
stored; in fact, it would not surprise me at all if these kinds of
experiences turn out to be just more or less random constructions of
the brain which are "patched in" to consciousness in such a way that
they are perceived as being true memories.  In other words, they may be
introduced "downstream" from where true memories are retrieved, hence
they are uncritically perceived by consciousness.

On the other hand, maybe some of these have been checked out and shown
to be true memories.  I would think someone would do a followup on
this, but I have never heard of it.

Thoughts?

Mark_Muhlestein@novell.com


