Date: Thu, 13 May 93 19:21:10 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Bits per second into the brain?

Mike Price, commenting on Brian Hawthorne's comment about opening
one's eyes for a split second and then recalling the scene:

> Do we recall images, or do we *think* we recall images?  I remember (!)
> reading of an experiment where yellow and blue objects (vehicles, as it
> happened) were deliberately blurred in the memory of test subjects.  The
> experimenters found that in subjects to whom it was suggested that
> they'd only seen one such object (colour unspecified) a significant
> number recalled it's colour as green.  They added the colours together
> as part of the data compression process.  Our memories undergo
> successive stages of data compression with time and are unpacked when
> recalled.  Which is why it's possible to recall, in visual detail,
> incidents that never happened.
> > 
> > The one-bit-per-second claim is not only not backed up by research,
> > but it is easily proven false by any human.
> 
> I have seen documentary evidence for this claim (although I think the
> claim has an error/subject range of 1-2 bits/second into long term
> storage).  If necessary I can find the references (I remember where to
> look!).

I look forward to seeing more information on this point. Is it a few
bits per second *on average* or a few bits per second *peak rate*? If
more bits per second are possible, at peak data input times, is there
a limit on how long this high data acquisition rate can continue?

(I don't believe in the folk notion that there's some finite
capacity--at least not that it's reachable easily--beyond which new
info is either rejected or "crowds out" earlier memories.)

Mike's point about "remembering the memory" is a good one, as we may be
compressing input into "basis vectors" made up of other, previous
memories. This seems quite likely.

Some miscellaneous points, offered with no references. (Perhaps, as my
colleage Perry often says, "Sounds like urban legend.")

1. Around 1970, I read in "Scientific American" about an interesting
experiment in eidetic memory. A man was shown one half of a random-dot
stereogram, consisting of a milllion dots or so. This was just for a
few seconds. Then he was shown another one. He was asked to mentally
fuse the two images. He did so and discovered the "message" (some
shape or symbol, I can't recall the details, ironically). The few
million dots were thus remembered, if this is true, for at least a few
minutes.

2. Those with photographic memories (can read passages at high speed
and then recite them from memory without error) are apparently
inputting bits at something like 10,000 bits per second. Since they
recite flawlessly, it is said, the "compression" (Mike's "remembering
the memory" point) seems minimal, or at least nonlossy.

(Caveat: I have also heard that "photographic memories" are a myth.
Certainly they're rare, but a myth?)

3. Autistic children, as shown convincingly on "60 Minutes," can in
some cases hear a piece of music and then play it flawlessly...this is
certainly more than a few bits per second (and I'm only counting notes
per second, not raw audio bandwidth, which may not be what is being
remembered). Many non-autistic people I know, but not me!, can recall
songs, poems, paintings, etc., from just brief exposure. (Yes, they
may be compressing the input by decomposing the raw bitstream into
"words" or "basis vectors." It's still impressive. And, arguably, even
if compression is occurring, it's still a higher data rate than the
few bits per second often cited.)

4. One derivation I heard for the 1-2 bits per second figure came from
studies of how long it took people to remember sequences of digits. I
hope the evidence for the 1-2 bps is based on more than this. (Humans
didn't need to remember sequences of digits to thrive, so it's not
surprising that our skills are more auditory, visual, geometric and
less numerical and algebraic.)

I will bet any single person out there $5 that the body of evidence
supports a data rate into longterm memory (> 1 week), sustainable over
a period of at least an hour, of more than 100 bits per second.

(If anybody is serious about this Hansonian challenge, issues need to
be ironed out. Who makes the decision? What error rate is allowable?
Etc. Still, this is potentially a well-formed challenge.)

Anyway, I'm pleased to see this topic, raised as I recall by Carf F. and
contributed to by many people. Getting a handle on this oft-cited
"bandwith of the brain" topic seems important.


