Date: Thu, 13 May 93 23:01:17 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Drake Equation and Modelling of the Unknown

Perry writes:

> Indeed, the real problem with the Drake equation is its assumption
> that the variables are independant, when they could easily be argued
> to be very dependant on each other.

I agree.  The Drake equation (could someone post it here, with a list of
the factors?) has a lot of implicit assumptions.  Robin suggested that
such equations help make your assumptions more explicit, and maybe they
do, but in this case there is a lot hidden.

Do you really find the expected number of civilizations in the galaxy by
multiplying together the number of planets that have life times the
probability that life will evolve intelligence times the probability that
the intelligence will create a civilization times the fraction of the
galaxy lifetime that civilizations last?  What about civilizations that
spread?  What about ones that rise and then fall and rise again?  What
about more complex phenomena, like directed panspermia, or special
creation?

But my other point is that I don't think we're in a position to estimate
things like the probability that life evolves on an earthlike planet.
Nor are we even in a very good position to estimate the chances that a star
system will have an earthlike planet.  Even less are we in a position
to estimate the chance that life will develop intelligence, or
intelligence will develop civilizations.  I don't really think error
bars suffice in this situation.

Take the origin of life.  We have a lot of theories about it, mostly
mutually exclusive, and little basis for choosing among them.  We've
had whole threads on this topic.  The nature of planetary systems is
another area that is very, very poorly understood.  As for the chances of
life going on to develop an intelligent civilization, this is not discussed
in any scientific field that I am aware of.

What these three situations have in common is that we have just the one
example of ourselves - just one earthlike planet, and look, it developed
life and that life developed a civilization.  What information does this
give you?  How do you extract probabilities from this?  I don't think it
can be done.

The Drake equation reminds me of the man who deduced from the Bible that
the Earth was formed on Tuesday, April 13, 6004 B.C. (or whenever).  This
is a figure which gives a false air of precision from some very fuzzy
data.

Similarly, I feel that the only accurate answer we can give to the
question of how many civilizations exist in the average Milky-Way type
galaxy is "we really don't have any idea".  Saying "2.4" is so misleading
that I would call it wrong (even if that is what happens to come out of
the Drake equation using somebody's guesses), and giving an estimate
which includes a span of a dozen orders of magnitude is ludicrous, IMO,
because it is useless for any practical purpose.

Hal

