Date: Thu, 13 May 93 23:25:44 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: TECH: Numerical Speculation

From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
> 
> As you might expect, I think it would be great to form consensus on
> such estimates through betting markets.  Such markets could create
> probability distributions over such parameters (eg # alien civils,
> bits/brain).  If you thought the consensus was too certain, you could
> buy assets near the tails of the distribution.  Thus a social process
> could correct for overconfidence, and still return numbers, even under
> very high uncertainty.  

Yes, I guess options do something like this for the stock market,
although as I understand it they are more useful for exposing volatility
estimates.  Perhaps that is due to their short-term nature.

I think the problem I see in these difficult problems with wide error
bars is that we really don't have enough information to form a decent
estimate.  There is a paradox in probability theory that applies here, I
think.  I don't remember it that well so I can't present my argument as
sharply as I'd like, but here is a first cut:

I take two coins and lay them on the table either heads or tails,
choosing how each will lie.  I then cover them, and we run a betting
market on whether each coin is heads or tails, as well as the total
number of heads exposed.  Later I will reveal the coins and we will
settle the betting.

What will the market do in this situation?  Equal odds of 50-50 for each
coin?  What about the number of heads?  If we assumed independence the
odds of 1 head are twice that of 0 or 2.  But that's not a valid
assumption.  So are 0, 1, and 2 equally probable?  How does that square
with the 50-50 odds for each coin?  How should a rational person bet?

I don't really know the answer, but the problem here is the same one I
see with the Drake equation or the neuron estimates - if you have no
information you can't come up with numerical estimates.  Attempting to do
so faces you with this paradox.  I'd be interested in more information
about this paradox if anyone has it.

Hal

