Date: Thu, 13 May 93 23:37:26 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Bits per second into the brain?

Tim May writes:

> I will bet any single person out there $5 that the body of evidence
> supports a data rate into longterm memory (> 1 week), sustainable over
> a period of at least an hour, of more than 100 bits per second.
> 
> (If anybody is serious about this Hansonian challenge, issues need to
> be ironed out. Who makes the decision? What error rate is allowable?
> Etc. Still, this is potentially a well-formed challenge.)

I'd be inclined to take Tim up on his challenge.  100 bits per second for
one hour is 360,000 bits or 45,000 bytes, about 9,000 words (I realize Tim
didn't reveal what input modality he was thinking of).  That's a lot to
memorize in an hour.  And I suspect that other modalities are going to
be equally difficult.

I would want to stipulate that we are talking about an average person,
not some genius with a photographic memory, and that the information
that goes in and comes out be new, and that it not be compressible by
computer to less than 360,000 bits.  For text I would be satisifed by
having the person study it for an hour then type it back out (or recite
it) a week later.  For music it could be the same - an hour of study,
then play it back on some instrument a week later.  (Remember, though,
that I would want to compress the music to make sure it is > 360,000
bits.  Music scores might be pretty compressible, although obviously for
five bucks I'm not going to make a career of finding the best music
compression.)

Visual input is harder to imagine measuring, since we have no good ways
of outputting graphics.  I suppose we could imagine someone studying a
photograph and reproducing it by painting a week later, but it's hard
to quantify the amount of information coming out.  Maybe we could do
fractal or other lossy image compression on the original picture, until
the compressed image was judged to be about as close to the original
the painting.  This would be a rough estimate of how much information
about the picture was captured.

Another approach would be to ask them questions about the picture, but
asking 360,000 bits of questions seems difficult.

Lots of issues here, but any way you cut it this seems like a lot of
information to cram into the brain in an hour.

Hal

