Date:     Fri, 14 May 93 8:44:06 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Personics

I was very disappointed when Personics failed.  I think it did so because it
missed the niche between purchasing a whole tape and a single or two.

Since tapes cost about $8.00, anyone that likes, say, 4 songs off of one will
buy the whole tape if the songs cost at least $2.00 each, assuming economic
rationality.  If someone just wants one or two songs, they'll just buy the
single (if available), and tape it into a song cycle if they want to on their
own equipment.

The niche Personics would have been best suited to was one for obscure singles
and for the sort of thing I wanted in an Oingo Boingo tape: my own personal
"best of," a few songs off off a few of their discs.  The reason they didn't
fill this niche is that they simply didn't have the selection.  It was good,
but inadequate.

They seemed to go for songs that had been released as singles; people that
wanted those could just buy the singles.  They should have included more
songs that had only been distributed on full-length discs.

