 RAYMOND KAMMER, National Institute of Standards and 
Technology: Under conditions where law enforcement authorities have 
a legal right to intercept the information, which is going to be a court 
order, then they would be able to understand the information.

 O'BRIEN: Officials say wiretaps lead to about 2,500 criminal 
convictions each year in the U.S.  In 1987, wiretaps by the FBI helped 
convict members of Chicago's El Rukin [sp?] street gang of plotting to 
commit terrorist acts on behalf of Libya.  Wiretaps also played an 
important role in cracking the Pentagon procurement fraud and bribery 
case in 1989.

 The new secure phone system is based on a computer chip called a 
clipper chip. AT&T got on the clipper chip bandwagon immediate, 
announcing that it would convert its scrambling devices to use clipper 
chips.  But some of the major computer makers in the U.S. have joined 
with public interest groups to express concerns about the clipper chip.

 Mr. ROTENBERG: Because of the secrecy surrounding the 
development of this particular standard, a lot of people are scratching 
their head and saying, 'Well, they say it does this, but how do we know 
that?'

 O'BRIEN: Both critics and supporters of the clipper chip suggest it may 
be time for a wide-open public debate about how to protect privacy 
without hampering law enforcement.

